Kreative Studio pose editor (STU-PE1). Plain JavaScript, no libraries, no build step. The same file runs in Node 22 (CommonJS require) and in the browser (classic <script>, no modules). Use exactly this wrapper and nothing outside it:

(function () {
  'use strict';
  const NS = typeof module === 'object' && module.exports ? null : (globalThis.KKPose = globalThis.KKPose || {});
  const need = (name, file) => (NS ? NS[name] : require(file));
  // ... the code; get other pose modules with need('vec', './vec.js') INSIDE functions (load order in the browser is free) ...
  const api = { /* exported names */ };
  if (NS) NS.<NAME> = api; else module.exports = api;
})();

Angles are degrees in every API. Coordinates are metres: +X is the figure's left, -Y its front, +Z up. Matrices are arrays of 9 numbers, row-major. Do not change the test file.


Write public/js/pose/ik.js (NAME = ik), analytic two-bone IK for arms and legs. It returns joint values; it never changes the pose it gets. Use need('vec','./vec.js'), need('skeleton','./skeleton.js') and need('fk','./fk.js') inside the functions (V, S, FK below).

Export CHAINS = { l_arm: {joints:['l_shoulder','l_elbow','l_wrist'], sign:1}, r_arm: {joints:['r_shoulder','r_elbow','r_wrist'], sign:1}, l_leg: {joints:['l_hip','l_knee','l_ankle'], sign:-1}, r_leg: {joints:['r_hip','r_knee','r_ankle'], sign:-1} } (in this key order), and solve(pose, {chain, target, pole}).

solve(pose, {chain, target, pole}), with [u, m, e] = CHAINS[chain].joints (upper, middle, end) and sign = CHAINS[chain].sign:
1. w = FK.solve(pose). A = w.pos[u]. la = V.len(rest(m) - rest(u)), lb = V.len(rest(e) - rest(m)) (rest = S.byName[name].rest).
2. full = V.len(target - A); reached = full >= |la - lb| && full <= la + lb. d = full clamped to [|la - lb| + 1e-4, la + lb - 1e-4]. dir = V.norm(target - A).
3. Pole: when pole is not given, pole = A + V.matVec(w.rot[P], off) where P = S.byName[u].parent, off = [0, 0.3, -0.4] for arms (behind and below) and [0, -0.4, -0.3] for legs (in front and below). side = (pole - A) - dir * dot(pole - A, dir); if V.len(side) < 1e-9 use side = V.matVec(w.rot[P], [0, -1, 0]) with the same projection. side = V.norm(side).
4. cosA = (la*la + d*d - lb*lb) / (2*la*d), sinA = sqrt(max(0, 1 - cosA*cosA)). E = A + la*(cosA*dir + sinA*side) (the new middle joint), T = A + d*dir (the end point actually reached).
5. Upper joint. Rp = w.rot[P]. r0 = V.norm(rest(m) - rest(u)); upperDir = V.norm(E - A); r = V.matVec(V.transpose(Rp), upperDir). L1 = V.rotBetween(r0, r). The middle joint's hinge is column 0 of FK.frame(m): hinge0 = [F[0], F[3], F[6]]. hingeNow = V.matVec(Rp, V.matVec(L1, hinge0)). want = V.scale(V.norm(V.cross(E - A, T - E)), sign). phi = V.signedAngle(hingeNow, want, upperDir). L = V.matMul(V.axisAngle(r, phi), L1).
6. Middle joint. Ru = V.matMul(Rp, L). f0 = V.norm(rest(e) - rest(m)); f = V.matVec(V.transpose(Ru), V.norm(T - E)). Lm = V.rotBetween(f0, f).
7. Turn each local matrix into joint values: toValues(name, L) = e = V.matToEuler(B^T * L * B) with B = FK.frame(name); for names starting with 'r_' negate e.side and e.twist; return {bend, side, twist}. (This is the inverse of FK.local.)
8. Return {joints: {[u]: toValues(u, L), [m]: toValues(m, Lm)}, reached}.
Throw new Error('chain must be l_arm, r_arm, l_leg or r_leg.') for an unknown chain and new Error('target must be [x, y, z].') when target is not an array of 3 finite numbers.
